IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- M4 (spec §10, R8 §8.8): `MAYBE NUMBER` and `MAYBE DATE` via PAIRED
-- IS-KNOWN QUESTIONS — and `WHEN JUST <value>`, the payload-VALUE match.
--
-- This file is `not-ok/maybe-number.l4` and `not-ok/just-payload-pattern.l4`
-- flipped. Both were refused by name at M1 for the same reason, and M4 owes
-- both an answer.
--
-- WHAT R8 WAS AFRAID OF. A docassemble number field that the user leaves
-- blank does not submit as None: it submits as `0.0` (webapp
-- interview/views.py:1413-1414), an empty integer as `'0'` (:1397-1398) and
-- an empty date as `''` (:1374). Erase `MAYBE NUMBER` to `NUMBER` and
-- "I did not declare any income" becomes indistinguishable from "I declared
-- income of nothing" — two different legal positions with two different
-- consequences, collapsed into one number by a form widget.
--
-- THE DESIGN. One L4 field becomes TWO docassemble questions: a boolean
-- is-known flag, and the value itself carrying
-- `show if: {code: <known flag>}`. The `{code: …}` spelling is load-bearing
-- and not decorative: it is the only server-side one (parse.py:6316-6325), so
-- on the absence path the value variable is left genuinely UNDEFINED. Probed
-- end to end against 1.10.7; probed also is the failure mode WITHOUT the
-- guard, where a second consumer reading the value on the absence path makes
-- docassemble ASK the value question anyway and a blank submission arrives as
-- 0.0 — a fabricated answer, silently.
--
-- THE CASES BELOW ARE PAIRED ON PURPOSE. Read #EVAL 2 against #EVAL 3 (a
-- declared income of ZERO against no declaration at all), #EVAL 4 against
-- #EVAL 5 (a stale work history against never having worked), and #EVAL 6
-- against #EVAL 7 (a disclaimed declaration against an unanswered one). In
-- each pair one side is a real value and the other is absence, and the two
-- sides disagree about the outcome. Any lowering that conflates them fails
-- one of the pair.

DECLARE Claim
    HAS
      @desc Income the claimant declared for the assessment period, if any was declared at all
      `declared income` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      @desc The date the claimant last worked, if they have ever worked
      `date last worked` IS A MAYBE DATE
      @desc The first day of the qualifying period
      `the qualifying date` IS A DATE
      @desc Whether the claimant confirmed the declaration, if they answered at all
      `declaration confirmed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN

@export Whether the claim must be referred for manual assessment
GIVEN c IS A Claim @desc The claim under assessment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claim must be referred` c IF
       `the declared income is above the threshold`
    OR `no income was declared at all`
    OR `the work history is stale`
    OR `the declaration was positively disclaimed`
 WHERE
    -- MAYBE NUMBER, present branch.
    `the declared income is above the threshold` MEANS
        CONSIDER c's `declared income`
        WHEN JUST n  THEN n GREATER THAN 1000
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

    -- MAYBE NUMBER, absent branch: absence is itself a referral ground, and
    -- a declared income of zero is NOT.
    `no income was declared at all` MEANS
        CONSIDER c's `declared income`
        WHEN JUST n  THEN FALSE
        WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE

    -- MAYBE DATE. Absence is not staleness: someone who has never worked has
    -- no stale work history. If the absent path arrived as `''`, this
    -- comparison would raise TypeError against a DADateTime — loudly, but it
    -- would still be wrong.
    `the work history is stale` MEANS
        CONSIDER c's `date last worked`
        WHEN JUST d  THEN d LESS THAN (c's `the qualifying date`)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

    -- `WHEN JUST FALSE` — a match on the payload VALUE, not a binder. The
    -- three states are distinct here and the rule uses all three: a
    -- positively disclaimed declaration is a referral ground, an unanswered
    -- one is not. M1's presence-erasure lowering could not express this and
    -- refused it by name; before that repair it compiled to `is None` and
    -- reported TRUE for the unanswered case.
    `the declaration was positively disclaimed` MEANS
        CONSIDER c's `declaration confirmed`
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

-- 1. Income declared above the threshold. The first disjunct decides;
--    everything after it is short-circuited away.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS JUST 2500
            `date last worked`      IS JUST (YMD 2024 6 1)
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS JUST TRUE
-- TRUE

-- 2. A REAL ZERO. Income was declared, and it was nothing. Not referable.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS JUST 0
            `date last worked`      IS JUST (YMD 2024 6 1)
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS JUST TRUE
-- FALSE

-- 3. ABSENCE. No income was declared at all. Referable. Read against #EVAL 2:
--    a lowering that lets a blank number field arrive as 0.0 answers this
--    FALSE, and a lowering that erases the MAYBE answers #EVAL 2 TRUE.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS NOTHING
            `date last worked`      IS JUST (YMD 2024 6 1)
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS JUST TRUE
-- TRUE

-- 4. A stale work history: last worked before the qualifying period opened.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS JUST 500
            `date last worked`      IS JUST (YMD 2019 3 4)
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS JUST TRUE
-- TRUE

-- 5. ABSENT DATE: never worked. Not stale, therefore not referable on that
--    ground. The `the qualifying date` question decides nothing here.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS JUST 500
            `date last worked`      IS NOTHING
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS JUST TRUE
-- FALSE

-- 6. The declaration was positively disclaimed: `WHEN JUST FALSE`.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS JUST 500
            `date last worked`      IS JUST (YMD 2024 6 1)
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS JUST FALSE
-- TRUE

-- 7. The declaration was never answered. Read against #EVAL 6: `NOTHING` is
--    not `JUST FALSE`, and the pre-repair `is None` lowering answered this
--    TRUE.
#EVAL `the claim must be referred` OF
        Claim WITH
            `declared income`       IS JUST 500
            `date last worked`      IS JUST (YMD 2024 6 1)
            `the qualifying date`   IS YMD 2024 1 1
            `declaration confirmed` IS NOTHING
-- FALSE
